You are a concise, capable assistant. Answer directly — skip filler, disclaimers, and preamble. When you show code, make it runnable.

{{stable}}

<!--
This is a STARTER system prompt for qchat. Copy it to ~/.config/qchat/system.md
(or run `/system edit` in the REPL) and make it yours.

Available {{tokens}} — substituted fresh on every request:
  {{stable}}    session-stable grounding: date (day) + location. Cache-safe; the default.
  {{date}}      e.g. "Wednesday, 2026-08-19"           (day resolution — stable within a session)
  {{location}}  from `/location` or settings.json      (empty string if unset)
  {{os}}        platform (Linux/Darwin/…)
  {{shell}}     $SHELL
  {{cwd}}       current working directory
  {{time}}      e.g. "20:14 PDT"   } LIVE clock — changes every turn, so putting these in the
  {{datetime}}  full date + time   } system prompt invalidates the prompt cache (SAINT breakpoints
  {{tz}}        e.g. "PDT"         } the system message). Prefer the get_current_time tool for a
  {{grounding}} date + minute time } live clock. Use these only if you accept the cache cost.

Unknown {{tokens}} and literal { } braces (e.g. in code examples) are left untouched.

Advanced — cache-safe volatile context (SAINT seats): text you place after a line
containing  <<<saint:volatile>>>  is treated by SAINT as per-turn volatile context. SAINT
strips the marker and moves that text past the prompt-cache breakpoints, so a live value
doesn't invalidate the cache. Example:

    You are a concise assistant. {{stable}}
    <<<saint:volatile>>>
    Right now it is {{datetime}}.

On the saint/cloud-* seats SAINT relocates it (cache-safe); on a direct local seat qchat
just drops the marker and inlines the text. For a live clock, the get_current_time tool is
usually the better choice than pinning it into the prompt.

Delete this comment block once you've customized the prompt — it's sent to the model as-is.
